Right back to when wine was made by crushing a bunch of grapes and
letting the yeasts naturally existing on the grape skins do their thing?

Fermentation is a fairly simple process.  Distillation's a tad trickier
(or least more immediately hazardous; evaporated alcohol's rather
flammable) but as long as you restrict yourself to fermenting sugars you
should be fine.

Heating should be done mildly at most once the must is set, and after
that you get the best product by fermenting in a cooler area.  Filtering
is only needed if you don't rack adequately or correctly.  I've never
had a need to stir after the must is set (and in fact I don't see why
you'd want to).

You probably need to keep things *clean*; foreign material can spoil the
product.  Thankfully, if that happens you'll likely end up with
something you wouldn't *want* to drink -- sour, moldy (I've seen this
one happen, believe it or not; the contaminant ended up killing the
yeast), and so on.


I've only brewed beer, wine, and mead though.  If you're talking about
liquors and spirits, that involves distillation and *that* I can believe
is more hazardous, both immediately (blow y'self up) and with the
product itself.


